  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study compares training losses across architectural variants and ablations, bearing on whether architecture details change loss only slightly. It does not fit or test scaling laws.",
    "gate_note": "The reported loss comparisons are ablations and architecture comparisons, mostly at 385M and 100K updates; the text says it averages losses from the last 1k iterations but does not state that these are held-out losses. The paper does not sweep parameter count, tokens, or compute to fit scaling laws, test large-run extrapolation, or identify a compute-optimal allocation. Benchmark evaluations are not reported as tests of predictable downstream scaling.",
    "test_reason": "The paper introduces a new linear-attention architecture (TransNormerLLM). It cites Kaplan et al. and Hoffmann et al. only in related work. It fits no scaling law and sweeps no parameters, tokens or compute toward one. Its loss tables are design ablations and comparisons of its own method against alternatives at one or two sizes. They do not vary depth or width at fixed parameter count, which is what V4 names. They swap components (attention type, positional encoding, gate, activation, norm) to choose the authors' own design. By the rules these are 'own method compared with others', not a test of V4. The reader's T2/tests classification and its V4 labels should be dropped.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Partly right. The authors conclude that TransNormerLLM consistently outperforms Transformers in both accuracy and efficiency. They say their ablations validate each modification (positional encoding, gating, activations, normalization, Lightning Attention). They report that the 385M/1B/7B models match leading Transformer LLMs on benchmarks while being faster in training and inference.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates architectural alternatives and ablations rather than fitting or extrapolating scaling laws; reported loss comparisons are mainly at 385M and 100K updates.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported loss varies with positional encoding and architecture choice; the paper describes several other tested ablations as having minimal or negligible impact.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "At matched size and 100K updates, swapping softmax attention for the authors' linear attention changes training loss by about 5% at 385M and 8-9% at 1B (Table 1). Component ablations (positional encoding, gate, activations, norm) change loss by 0-6% at 385M, with APE the largest (2.387 vs 2.248).",
        "proposed_claim": "At fixed parameter count, the choice of token-mixing mechanism and positional encoding changes LM training loss by several percent, more than shape choices are claimed to.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
          "r9"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/OpenNLPLab/TransnormerLLM",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "The loss comparisons are concentrated in small architectural ablations, mostly at 385M and 100K updates. The paper does not identify these losses as held-out, reports no uncertainty for the table values, and provides no scaling-law fits.",
    "rows_left_out": "No relevant loss-ablation rows were left out. Downstream benchmark scores and efficiency measurements were not included because they do not test the versions that this study bears on.",
